<!--Blurb: Don’t spend too much time teaching others when you still have work to do.-->
<h2 class="h1">Ānanda<br/>
Ānanda Sutta  (SN 9:5)</h2>
<p>On one occasion Ven. Ānanda was staying among the Kosalans in a forest grove. Now at that time, he was spending too much time devoted to instructing laypeople.</p>
<p>Then the devatā inhabiting the forest grove, feeling sympathy for Ven. Ānanda, desiring his benefit, desiring to bring him to his senses, approached him and recited a verse:</p>
<div class="verse">
<pre>“Going to the bower
at the root of a tree,
placing
		unbinding
in your heart,
do jhāna, Gotama,
&amp; don’t be heedless.
	What use is this
	chitter-chatter
	to you?”</pre>
</div><!--end verse-->
<p>Then Ven. Ānanda, chastened by the devatā, came to his senses.</p>
<p class="seealso">See also: <a href="../KN/Thag/thag1_119.html" class="sutta-ref">Thag 1:119</a></p>
      </div><!--end:sutta-->
